## Comparison Table

| Feature | Batch | Time-Sharing | Distributed | Network | Real-Time |
|---------|-------|--------------|-------------|---------|-----------|
| **User Interaction** | None | Yes | Minimal | Yes | Minimal |
| **Response Time** | High | Medium | Medium | Medium | Very Low |
| **Complexity** | Low | Medium | High | Medium | High |
| **Resource Sharing** | Limited | Excellent | Excellent | Excellent | Limited |
| **Cost** | Low | Medium | High | Medium-High | High |
| **Scalability** | Low | Medium | High | High | Low |
| **Fault Tolerance** | Low | Medium | High | High | High |

---

## Summary

Each type of operating system is designed with specific requirements and use cases in mind:

- **Batch OS**: For high-volume, non-interactive processing
- **Time-Sharing OS**: For multiple concurrent users
- **Distributed OS**: For networked, scalable systems
- **Network OS**: For resource sharing and centralized management
- **Real-Time OS**: For time-critical applications

Choosing the right OS depends on your application requirements, performance expectations, cost constraints, and the environment in which it will operate.

